I have confirmed the root cause from both the code and the actual run log. No further reads needed.

## Root cause (confirmed)

**The bring-up loop has zero cross-project awareness of `container_name:`.** It iterates `plan.composeDirs` in order and runs `docker compose up -d` on each independently — `/opt/nxeon/worker/migrate-real.ts:5363-5417`.

The log for run `cmsciv67q0005hlhwo5vzzu7h` shows exactly the predicted sequence:
- `[01:02:15] Bringing up 4 Docker Compose project(s).`
- `/opt/soulpilates` → **3 services running** (it went first, so it created `/soulpilates-mongo`).
- `/opt/soulpilates-staging` → **no service stayed running**, with the raw daemon error:
  `Conflict. The container name "/soulpilates-mongo" is already in use by container "bf9de2e043c1…"`

So `/opt/soulpilates` (prod) wins the name; `/opt/soulpilates-staging` collides because both compose files hardcode `container_name: soulpilates-mongo`, and compose treats that as an absolute name (no `<project>_` prefix).

**Why it worked on the source:** the migration brings up *every* `composeDir` unconditionally, in the same order, on one host. The source almost certainly did not have both stacks running simultaneously under the same container names — staging was stopped (or its names differed and were changed to match prod at some point). The engine never checks the source per-project running state for bring-up; it starts them all, so the two clones collide on the target for the first time.

**Current failure handling** (`5399-5416`): `up -d` returns non-zero, then after a 10s settle `runningCount` (5405-5406) is 0, so it takes the `else` branch (5410) and reports the raw `lastErrorLine(err…)` — i.e. it surfaces the daemon Conflict string as a generic "containers did not stay up." There is **no** duplicate-`container_name` detection anywhere (`grep` confirms `container_name` appears nowhere in the file; the only cross-project awareness is the mount-based `underComposeDir` at 5439-5445, which is for standalone containers, not compose name clashes).

## Recommended safest behavior

Exactly your lean, and it is the safe one: **detect duplicate `container_name:` across `composeDirs` before bring-up; the first project to claim a name owns it; any later project sharing ≥1 name is skipped with a precise, honest message.** Do **not** auto-rewrite the customer's compose file, and do **not** inject a container_name-stripping override (that changes app expectations — inter-service DNS, external references to `soulpilates-mongo`, etc.).

## Concrete minimal fix (file:line)

Reuse the loop that already exists — no separate pre-pass needed. Two small additions in `/opt/nxeon/worker/migrate-real.ts`.

**1. Declare an ownership map just before the loop** (at line 5365, right after the `report(74, …)` on 5364):

```ts
// container_name: in a compose file is an ABSOLUTE name (no <project>_ prefix),
// so two migrated projects that hardcode the same one (e.g. an app's prod + its
// staging clone) collide on one host: whoever runs first wins the name, the next
// gets a raw "Conflict. The container name … is already in use". Track who owns
// each name and skip later clones with an honest step instead of that daemon error.
const claimedNames = new Map<string, string>(); // container_name -> owning dir
```

**2. Insert the clash check right before `const run = …` (currently line 5387), i.e. after the missing-vars `continue` (5383) so only projects we actually intend to start participate:**

```ts
// Read the literal container_name values this project pins.
const names = clean(
  (await sh(conn,
    `grep -hE '^[[:space:]]*container_name:[[:space:]]*' ${ce.composeFile} 2>/dev/null` +
    ` | sed -E 's/.*container_name:[[:space:]]*//; s/[[:space:]]*#.*$//; s/^["'\'']//; s/["'\'']$//'`
  )).stdout, 2048
).split("\n").map((s) => s.trim()).filter(Boolean);
const clash = names.find((n) => claimedNames.has(n));
if (clash) {
  const owner = claimedNames.get(clash)!;
  push({
    step: `Compose up ${dir}`,
    status: "skipped",
    detail:
      `not started — it reuses the fixed container name "${clash}" already claimed by ${owner}. ` +
      `A compose file's container_name is an absolute name, and Docker will not run two containers with ` +
      `that name on one host. These are the same app's separate stacks (prod + staging), which the old ` +
      `server did not run side by side. To start it here: run it on a separate host, or give it unique ` +
      `container_name values in ${ce.composeFile}, then: cd ${dir} && ${composeCmd} ${composeFileArg(ce.composeFile)}${ce.args}up -d`,
  });
  await report(76, "Starting containers…", `  ${dir}: skipped — reuses container name "${clash}" owned by ${owner}.`);
  continue;
}
for (const n of names) claimedNames.set(n, dir);
```

That's the whole change: ~20 lines, no new helpers (`sh`, `clean`, `push`, `report`, `composeFileArg`, `ce` are all already in scope in this block).

### Behavior after the fix
- `/opt/soulpilates` starts (unchanged), registers `soulpilates-mongo` (and any other pinned names).
- `/opt/soulpilates-staging` is now reported as **skipped** with a specific, actionable message naming the conflicting name and the owning dir — instead of a raw `Conflict …` daemon string dressed up as "containers did not stay up."
- The other two failures in this run are unrelated and unaffected (`studionexis` = missing image `studionexis/backend`; `mailserver` = external network `soulpilates_internal` not found).

### How container_name is read, and the tradeoff
The `grep` reads the compose file's **literal** `container_name:` lines (the file is already resolved into `ce.composeFile` by `composeEnvFor`, 5325-5357). This catches the real-world case here, where the name is a hardcoded string.

If you want to also catch names built from `${VAR}` interpolation, the precise alternative is:
`docker compose -f ${ce.composeFile} ${ce.args}config --format json` and read `.services[].container_name`. I would **not** default to that: `config` re-resolves the whole file and can fail on the same missing-var conditions already gated at 5371-5383, and adds a jq/JSON-parse dependency. Two prod/staging clones that derive the name from a var almost always set that var to the same value anyway (that is why they collide), so literal grep is the minimal, robust choice; `config --format json` is a strict upgrade only if you later see variable-derived collisions.

### One caveat on registration timing
The fix registers names at *attempt* time (right before `up -d`). Worst case — if the owning project's `up` fails to actually create the container (e.g. missing image) — a later clone is still skipped even though the name isn't truly taken. That errs safe: the user gets an exact command to run it manually, and never a confusing raw Docker conflict. If you want it airtight, register a name only after confirming it exists (`docker ps -a --format '{{.Names}}'` post-bring-up), but that is beyond the minimal fix and not needed to resolve this failure.
